@heading 
Contents 
@description 
How to design a plan to landscape your yard. 
@contents
     Successfully planting the space surrounding a house requires
careful and thoughtful planning, beginning by establishing how
this outside area will be used and then deciding what parts of
the yard will be allocated to ~private areas~, ~public areas~,
play areas, or ~sevice areas~.  Make a ~checklist of needs~ and
expectations for these areas.  The first step is to ~outline~ the
lot on paper and trace in the house and other architectual
features.  This rough shell will be used to develop the final
~landscape design~in stages; make several copies. Select views
from inside and outside the house that would be best ~screened
from view~ or might be~framed~.  Note existing vegetation. 
Juxapostion all of these things on the ~outline~ making ~rough
sketches~ of these areas. 
     Next consider physical characterists of the land.  Analyse
the site ~topography~, ~drainage~, present vegetation,
structures, walks and driveways, and existing ~natural features~.

Have a ~soil~sample tested by the ~County Extension Service~. 
Consider the ~orientation of the house~ and where the ~sun~ or
~shade~ would effect plantings.  Again, make ~rough sketches~ of
these characteristics on a clean ~outline~ of the lot and a
detailed ~checklist of physical features~.  
     Using the ~rough sketches~, ~lot outline~, and ~checklists~,
design a landscape plan on paper.  Start with the dominant
plants, the trees.  Placement and ~selection of trees~ is very
important although a small lot may need only one or two.  Use
trees to ~shade the house~, ~frame a view~ or hide less
attractive aspects of a yard or home.  ~Hardiness~, ~habit of
growth~, and the ~physical features of the lot~ will limit the
selection.  Weigh the ~pros and cons~ carefully before removing
existing trees.  ~Shrubs~ fill in a landscape.  They come in a
variety of ~form~, ~color~, ~texture~, and ~bloom~. 
Characteristics such as ~time of bloom~, ~environmental
requirements~, ~height~ and ~habit of growth~ will infuence the
choices available.  They can be used as ~specimen plants~,
~accent plants~, for ~hedges~, ~borders~ or ~group plantings~. 
~Ground covers~ unify plantings and lessen maintenance
requirements.
     Only after all this initial paperwork should the actual
selction of individual plants begin.  Decisions need to be made:
~evergreen~ or ~decidious~, shrub, tree or ~flower~. Every choice
should be linked with the ~rough sketches~, ~checklists~, uses
and ~environmental considerations~ which become apparent in the
prior planning of a landscape design.  Once again juxaposition
all ideas on the ~lot outline~and try to visualize what the
outside will look like.  On to the plant nursery at last!

